  "privacy_boundary": {
    "exact_rule": "Two-Part Absolute Ban: (1) No Person-Describing Facts—no entity, field, summary, or index in the base may identify, describe, evaluate, biographize, or store personal contact details (personal NetID/UNI email, personal phone, personal handle, DOB) for any natural person (student, faculty, staff, speaker, performer, or organizer). (2) No Behavioral or Relational Student Traces—no individual or Gemi query, growth need state, RSVP, physical presence, attendance count, rating, or person-to-person relationship may ever enter the base, whether raw or k-anonymized. Only institutions, spaces, impersonal needs, opportunities/works, organisations, role channels, and verified organisation AI agents may exist in the base.",
    "tricky_examples": [
      {
        "scenario": "1. Publicly published speaker names, bios, performers, and staff contacts on official .edu event pages (e.g., nyu-04 'Research Seminar: Alessandro Rizzo' + 'About the Speaker', nyu-09 'Event Organizer: Jenny Rossberg', nyu-15 jazz trio player names, col-01 'Kent Katner / 2128533960', col-07 'Emily Ailts / ea3101@columbia.edu').",
        "ruling": "REJECT all person fields, bios, and personal contacts; PERMIT opaque verbatim work/event titles only without person indexing. Specifically: speaker biographies (nyu-04), performer rosters (nyu-15), LibGuide authors ('by Laurie Murphy' in nyu-10), organizer names (nyu-09), staff phones (col-01), and personal UNI emails ('ea3101@columbia.edu' in col-07) are scrubbed before write, while institutional role inboxes ('cusp.education@nyu.edu', 'zotero-help@library.columbia.edu', 'mpap-concertmusic@nyu.edu') are kept. If an official event title contains a name ('Research Seminar: Alessandro Rizzo' or 'City of Fortune -- A Book Talk with Mason B. Williams'), Event.title keeps the verbatim title solely so students can match campus signage, but no speaker field, Person node, or person-lookup index is created."
      },
      {
        "scenario": "2. Covering 'mentors' and 'research openings' in Domain 3 when students equate mentorship and lab openings with specific professors or upperclassmen.",
        "ruling": "REJECT individual mentor profiles; MODEL mentorship and research strictly as impersonal Opportunity/Service nodes run by an Organisation and fronted by a role Agent or P2P Gemi handshake. For example, 'Steinhardt Peer Mentorship Program' or 'CUSP Urban Informatics Research Opening' enters the base linked to Organisation:CUSP and its Agent. If a professor or upperclassman mentors in Gemiverse, a student's Gemi discovers the impersonal Opportunity in the base, initiates a direct P2P conversation with the lab Agent or mentor's Gemi, and—upon mutual consent—stores the human mentor relationship strictly in each Gemi's private name cards."
      },
      {
        "scenario": "3. Writing 'anonymous' Gemi crowd signals back to the base—such as '11 Gemis plan to attend Finals Study Break' or 'Gemis report Bobst Rm 743 is loud right now'.",
        "ruling": "REJECT completely. Even unnamed Gemi counts leak individual presence and private needs in small rooms or niche clubs (e.g., count=1 in a seminar room exposes the lone student present) and introduce uncheckable subjective gossip into a public-fact graph. Only official institutional public feeds (e.g., an official university library sensor API on .edu or Bedework's 'WAIT-LIST-LIMIT: 0' in col-05) may enter the base; friend coordination ('who among my friends is going?') happens strictly P2P using private name cards."
      }
    ]
